---
import CaseStudy from "../../layouts/CaseStudy.astro";
import CaseSection from "../../components/CaseSection.astro";
import Prose from "../../components/Prose.astro";
import Figure from "../../components/Figure.astro";
import Glance from "../../components/Glance.astro";
import Callout from "../../components/Callout.astro";
---

<CaseStudy
  slug="scorecard-gamification"
  team="PM, EM & engineering (incl. architect)"
  readTime="7 min read"
  coverPlain
>
  <Glance challengeLabel="Challenge" strategyLabel="Solution" impactLabel="Outcome">
    <Fragment slot="challenge">
      Scorecards told teams how mature their services were, but the data alone
      didn't change behavior. Usage was low, and compliance managers had no
      way to say which rules mattered most or to rally teams around improving.
    </Fragment>
    <Fragment slot="strategy">
      A gamified layer on Scorecards: maturity levels, configurable weights, and
      a team leaderboard, fit to New Relic's rule-based model rather than the
      competitor pattern. I led the direction and aligned Engineering and Product
      leaders behind it, naming the trade-offs to earn buy-in.
    </Fragment>
    <Fragment slot="impact">
      Turns best-practice adoption from an administrative chore into a culture of
      ownership: teams can see how they level up, what to fix next, and how they
      rank against their peers.
    </Fragment>
  </Glance>

  <section class="mx-auto max-w-5xl px-6 sm:px-10">
    <Prose>
      <p>
        Scorecards is how New Relic customers hold their engineering organization
        to a bar: quality, security, ownership, production readiness. It measures
        services against rules the customer defines and rolls the results into a
        score. But a score on a dashboard doesn't, by itself, change how teams
        behave. Our data analysis showed usage was low, and customer conversations
        told us why: because every rule counted equally, compliance managers
        couldn't flag what carried the most risk, and engineering teams had no way
        to tell what to fix first.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Gamification</strong> is a direct and common way to solve problems
        like these. As the product designer, I designed that experience for
        Scorecards: <strong>maturity levels, weights, and a team leaderboard</strong>.
      </p>
    </Prose>
  </section>

  <CaseSection title="Who we designed for">
    <Prose>
      <p>
        The work served two roles: <strong>engineering leaders</strong> (primary)
        and <strong>compliance managers</strong> (secondary). Each pain point maps
        to a specific part of the solution:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Engineering leaders struggle with low standards adoption across
          teams.</strong> Levels and a team leaderboard turn improvement into
          visible progress and healthy competition.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Compliance managers can't express what matters most.</strong>{" "}
          Configurable weights let them prioritize the rules that carry the most
          risk, so a score reflects real priorities rather than counting every rule
          equally.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Leaders lack a read on where to invest.</strong> Maturity
          distribution and trends over time pinpoint the teams and services that
          need support to reach the next level.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </Prose>
  </CaseSection>

  <CaseSection title="Our approach">
    <Prose>
      <p>
        Rather than rebuild Scorecards to match how competitors do gamification,
        we layered it onto the model New Relic already had. That meant three
        connected moves —
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Maturity levels</strong> to give teams something to climb,</li>
        <li><strong>Weights</strong> to give rules different priorities, and</li>
        <li><strong>A leaderboard</strong> to turn progress into healthy competition.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        The hard part was fitting each onto a rule-based model that was never
        designed for leveling. The sections below walk through how.
      </p>
    </Prose>
  </CaseSection>

  <CaseSection num="01" title="How New Relic Scorecards actually work">
    <Prose>
      <p>
        Every design decision came back to one thing: how a scorecard actually
        gets scored. It works like this:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>A Scorecard is a set of rules.</li>
        <li>
          Each rule is a query that checks a group of entities (services,
          browser apps, and so on) and marks each one pass or fail.
        </li>
        <li>
          A rule's score is entities passed ÷ entities checked, and the
          scorecard's score is the average of its rules.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </Prose>

    <figure class="my-10">
      <div class="rounded-2xl border border-line bg-card/60 px-4 py-6 sm:px-6">
        <svg
          viewBox="0 0 760 300"
          class="mx-auto w-full max-w-2xl"
