          <!-- Annotations -->
          <text x="24" y="273" fill="#6c685f" font-size="11" font-family="ui-monospace, monospace">&#9679; pass&#160;&#160;&#9675; fail</text>
          <text x="212" y="273" fill="#6c685f" font-size="11" font-family="ui-monospace, monospace">rule = passed &#247; checked</text>
        </svg>
      </div>
      <figcaption class="mt-3 font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-wide text-muted">
        The scoring model: entities roll up into a rule score, rules average into a scorecard score
      </figcaption>
    </figure>
    <Prose>
      <p>
        It's a flexible, composable model: <strong>the same service can be
        measured against different criteria in different scorecards</strong>.
        Powerful for customers, and the reason leveling wasn't a simple add-on.
      </p>
    </Prose>
  </CaseSection>

  <CaseSection num="02" title="A more flexible model, a harder problem">
    <Prose>
      <h3>How competitors do it</h3>
      <p>
        Before designing anything, I benchmarked how the category already handles
        this, studying Cortex, Rely.io, and Port. Gamifying Scorecards centers on
        the idea of leveling: giving a service a named level it can climb, like
        Bronze, then Silver, then Gold, as it meets more of a scorecard's
        requirements. It's the established pattern, and all three do it the same
        clean way.
      </p>
      <p>
        In their model, a service{" "}
        <strong>attaches directly to a scorecard</strong> and climbs its levels as
        it passes more of that scorecard's checks. The level is an attribute of the
        entity, scoped to one scorecard.
      </p>
      <h3>Our data model works differently</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Entities aren't scored by a scorecard directly;{" "}
          <strong>they're scored by its rules</strong>.</li>
        <li>A service isn't simply "in" a scorecard the way it is in Cortex. It
          sits in the intersection of that scorecard's rules.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        And that flexibility is exactly the point. Scoring at the rule level is
        what lets customers orchestrate how they want to measure things: composing
        rules freely and reusing the same entities across scorecards.
      </p>
      <h3>The trade-off that made leveling hard</h3>
      <p>
        New Relic's flexibility has a flip side: there's no single "level" baked into a
        scorecard for an entity to climb. Adding a straightforward leveling layer
        on top of it was the real design problem. More freedom for the customer,
        less obvious a place to hang the gamification.
      </p>
    </Prose>
    <figure class="mt-8 mb-14">
      <div class="grid gap-5 sm:grid-cols-2">
        <!-- Competitor model -->
        <div class="rounded-2xl border border-line bg-card/60 p-6">
          <p class="font-mono text-[11px] uppercase tracking-wide text-muted">Competitors</p>
          <svg viewBox="0 0 360 210" class="mt-3 w-full" fill="none" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" font-family="ui-sans-serif, system-ui, sans-serif">
            <!-- Scorecard -->
            <rect x="120" y="14" width="120" height="38" rx="8" fill="#ffffff" stroke="#514d45" stroke-width="1.5"/>
            <text x="180" y="38" text-anchor="middle" font-size="13" font-weight="600" fill="#514d45">Scorecard</text>
            <!-- connectors -->
            <line x1="180" y1="52" x2="65" y2="130" stroke="#6c685f" stroke-width="1.5"/>
            <line x1="180" y1="52" x2="180" y2="130" stroke="#6c685f" stroke-width="1.5"/>
            <line x1="180" y1="52" x2="295" y2="130" stroke="#6c685f" stroke-width="1.5"/>
            <!-- entities carry a level -->
            <rect x="22" y="130" width="86" height="44" rx="8" fill="#f7f5f0" stroke="#d9d5cc"/>
            <circle cx="42" cy="152" r="5" fill="#b06b3a"/>
            <text x="54" y="156" font-size="12" fill="#514d45">Entity</text>
            <text x="65" y="169" text-anchor="middle" font-size="9" fill="#6c685f" font-family="ui-monospace, monospace">Bronze</text>
            <rect x="137" y="130" width="86" height="44" rx="8" fill="#f7f5f0" stroke="#d9d5cc"/>
            <circle cx="157" cy="152" r="5" fill="#9aa0a6"/>
            <text x="169" y="156" font-size="12" fill="#514d45">Entity</text>
            <text x="180" y="169" text-anchor="middle" font-size="9" fill="#6c685f" font-family="ui-monospace, monospace">Silver</text>
            <rect x="252" y="130" width="86" height="44" rx="8" fill="#f7f5f0" stroke="#d9d5cc"/>
            <circle cx="272" cy="152" r="5" fill="#c9a227"/>
            <text x="284" y="156" font-size="12" fill="#514d45">Entity</text>
            <text x="295" y="169" text-anchor="middle" font-size="9" fill="#6c685f" font-family="ui-monospace, monospace">Gold</text>
          </svg>
          <p class="mt-3 text-xs leading-relaxed text-muted">
            The entity attaches directly to a scorecard, so its level (Bronze,
            Silver, or Gold) is just an attribute there.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- New Relic model -->
        <div class="rounded-2xl border border-line bg-card/60 p-6">
          <p class="font-mono text-[11px] uppercase tracking-wide text-[#0041d9]">New Relic</p>
          <svg viewBox="0 0 360 210" class="mt-3 w-full" fill="none" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" font-family="ui-sans-serif, system-ui, sans-serif">
            <!-- Scorecard -->
            <rect x="120" y="10" width="120" height="36" rx="8" fill="#ffffff" stroke="#0041d9" stroke-width="1.5"/>
            <text x="180" y="33" text-anchor="middle" font-size="13" font-weight="600" fill="#0041d9">Scorecard</text>
            <!-- scorecard -> rules -->
            <line x1="180" y1="46" x2="65" y2="86" stroke="#6c685f" stroke-width="1.5"/>
            <line x1="180" y1="46" x2="180" y2="86" stroke="#6c685f" stroke-width="1.5"/>
            <line x1="180" y1="46" x2="295" y2="86" stroke="#6c685f" stroke-width="1.5"/>
            <!-- rules -->
            <rect x="30" y="86" width="70" height="30" rx="7" fill="#ffffff" stroke="#514d45" stroke-width="1.5"/>
            <text x="65" y="105" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11" fill="#514d45">Rule</text>
            <rect x="145" y="86" width="70" height="30" rx="7" fill="#ffffff" stroke="#514d45" stroke-width="1.5"/>
            <text x="180" y="105" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11" fill="#514d45">Rule</text>
            <rect x="260" y="86" width="70" height="30" rx="7" fill="#ffffff" stroke="#514d45" stroke-width="1.5"/>
            <text x="295" y="105" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11" fill="#514d45">Rule</text>
            <!-- rules -> entities -->
            <line x1="65" y1="116" x2="65" y2="140" stroke="#6c685f" stroke-width="1.5"/>
            <line x1="180" y1="116" x2="180" y2="140" stroke="#6c685f" stroke-width="1.5"/>
            <line x1="295" y1="116" x2="295" y2="140" stroke="#6c685f" stroke-width="1.5"/>
            <!-- entity dots per rule -->
            <circle cx="50" cy="150" r="4.5" fill="#0041d9"/>
            <circle cx="65" cy="150" r="4.5" fill="#f2e8e3" stroke="#d9d5cc"/>
            <circle cx="80" cy="150" r="4.5" fill="#0041d9"/>
            <circle cx="165" cy="150" r="4.5" fill="#0041d9"/>
            <circle cx="180" cy="150" r="4.5" fill="#0041d9"/>
            <circle cx="195" cy="150" r="4.5" fill="#f2e8e3" stroke="#d9d5cc"/>
            <circle cx="280" cy="150" r="4.5" fill="#f2e8e3" stroke="#d9d5cc"/>
            <circle cx="295" cy="150" r="4.5" fill="#0041d9"/>
            <circle cx="310" cy="150" r="4.5" fill="#f2e8e3" stroke="#d9d5cc"/>
            <text x="180" y="182" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10" fill="#6c685f" font-family="ui-monospace, monospace">entities&#160;(&#9679;&#160;pass&#160;&#160;&#9675;&#160;fail)</text>
          </svg>
          <p class="mt-3 text-xs leading-relaxed text-muted">
            Entities are scored through rules, so there is no single entity level
            to attach a badge to.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <figcaption class="mt-3 font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-wide text-muted">
        Why leveling is straightforward for competitors, but not for New Relic
      </figcaption>
    </figure>
    <Callout label="The real question">
      New Relic's model is multi-dimensional: entity → rule → scorecard, then grouped by
      team or not at all. How do we surface leveling gracefully across every one
      of those dimensions, without overwhelming the interface?
    </Callout>
  </CaseSection>

  <CaseSection num="03" title="Designing how a team levels up">
    <Prose>
      <p>
        If entities are scored through rules, then the rule is the only place a
        level can actually live. So rather than attach a level to the scorecard as
        a whole, we attached it to each <strong>rule</strong>. Working with the PM,
        EM, and the team's architect, we designed a model that layered leveling
        onto the existing rule structure instead of fighting it:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          Each rule in a scorecard is assigned to a <strong>maturity level</strong>:
          Below basic, Basic, Intermediate, or Advanced.
        </li>
        <li>
          An entity's maturity level is the <strong>highest level whose rules it's
          passing</strong>, so progress is something a team can actually climb.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Weights</strong> let a compliance manager mark which rules matter
          most, so the score and the level reflect real priorities rather than
          treating every rule as equal.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        I designed the setup flow so an author can drag each rule into the level it
        belongs to. The other views build on that setup: the scorecard view shows
        its rules grouped by level, and the entity view shows how mature each
        service is and how they're spread across the levels.
      </p>
    </Prose>

    <Figure
      src="/images/scorecard-gamification/leveling-setup.mp4"
      caption="Setup: assigning each rule to a maturity level (Below basic → Advanced)"
      class="mx-auto max-w-2xl"
    />

    <div class="mt-10 grid gap-8 md:grid-cols-2 md:items-start">
      <Figure
        src="/images/scorecard-gamification/scorecard-detail.jpg"
        caption="Scorecard view: rules grouped by the maturity level they define"
        class="my-0"
      />
      <Figure
        src="/images/scorecard-gamification/entity-maturity.png"
        caption="Entity view: maturity distribution and per-entity progress"
        class="my-0"
      />
    </div>

    <Callout>
      Levels don't live on the scorecard; they live on its rules. That one move let
      leveling ride on top of New Relic's flexible model instead of requiring us to
      rebuild it.
    </Callout>
  </CaseSection>

  <CaseSection num="04" title="Designing how teams compare">
    <Prose>
      <p>
        Levels and weights answered <em>how a team makes progress</em>. The
        leaderboard asked a different question: when we crown a team, what exactly
        are we ranking? It's the piece that changed the most between my early
        explorations and what shipped.
      </p>
      <p>
        Most of my early directions leaned into richness, each trying a different
        way to pack in badges, levels, and per-rule detail:
      </p>
    </Prose>

    <div class="mt-8 grid grid-cols-2 gap-x-6 gap-y-8 sm:grid-cols-4">
        <div>
          <div class="mb-1.5 font-mono text-sm font-semibold text-[#0041d9]">A</div>
          <img
            src="/images/scorecard-gamification/leaderboard-expl-1-badgecounts.png"
            alt="Leaderboard with a podium and per-team badge counts"
            loading="lazy"
            class="w-full rounded-lg border border-line bg-white shadow-[0_6px_20px_-12px_rgba(16,24,40,0.3)]"
          />
        </div>
        <div>
          <div class="mb-1.5 font-mono text-sm font-semibold text-[#0041d9]">B</div>
          <img
            src="/images/scorecard-gamification/leaderboard-expl-2-named.png"
            alt="Leaderboard with named tier badges and rank numbers"
            loading="lazy"
            class="w-full rounded-lg border border-line bg-white shadow-[0_6px_20px_-12px_rgba(16,24,40,0.3)]"
          />
        </div>
        <div>
          <div class="mb-1.5 font-mono text-sm font-semibold text-[#0041d9]">C</div>
          <img
            src="/images/scorecard-gamification/leaderboard-expl-3-transparency.png"
            alt="Leaderboard encoding level through filled and faded badges, with per-rule detail"
            loading="lazy"
            class="w-full rounded-lg border border-line bg-white shadow-[0_6px_20px_-12px_rgba(16,24,40,0.3)]"
          />
        </div>
        <div>
          <div class="mb-1.5 font-mono text-sm font-semibold text-[#0041d9]">D</div>
          <img
            src="/images/scorecard-gamification/leaderboard-expl-4-barchart.png"
            alt="Leaderboard breaking every scorecard into per-level bar charts"
            loading="lazy"
            class="w-full rounded-lg border border-line bg-white shadow-[0_6px_20px_-12px_rgba(16,24,40,0.3)]"
          />
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="mt-5 grid gap-4 sm:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-4">
      <div class="rounded-2xl border border-line bg-card/60 p-4">
        <p class="font-display text-sm font-semibold text-[#0041d9]">A. Medals + badge counts</p>
        <p class="mt-1.5 text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted">
          A podium plus a row of badge counts per level. Showed breadth of
          achievement, but counting badges rewarded teams that simply owned more
          scorecards, and the row was busy to read.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="rounded-2xl border border-line bg-card/60 p-4">
        <p class="font-display text-sm font-semibold text-[#0041d9]">B. Named tier badges</p>
        <p class="mt-1.5 text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted">
          Memorable identities like “DevOps guru” or “The innovator.” Characterful,
          but the label needed decoding, and pairing it with rank numbers and
          scores was hard to correlate.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="rounded-2xl border border-line bg-card/60 p-4">
        <p class="font-display text-sm font-semibold text-[#0041d9]">C. Badge transparency</p>
        <p class="mt-1.5 text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted">
          Level encoded with filled vs. faded badges, expandable to per-rule
          detail. Precise, but the transparency states were subtle and the detail
          dense.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="rounded-2xl border border-line bg-card/60 p-4">
        <p class="font-display text-sm font-semibold text-[#0041d9]">D. Per-level bar charts</p>
        <p class="mt-1.5 text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted">
          Every scorecard broken into Basic / Bronze / Silver / Gold bars. The most
          detailed design, and the hardest to scan; it makes it hard to know who's
          ahead.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <Prose class="mt-14">
      <p>
        A quick round of internal testing made the pattern clear. Even engineering
        managers, the people closest to this data, struggled to connect the badges,
        icons, and numbers to what they actually meant.
      </p>
      <p>
        So I cut hard and made the leaderboard as simple and direct as I could: a
        podium for the top three, teams ranked by one{" "}
        <strong>team score</strong> on the same scale for everyone, and nothing
        else competing for attention. The per-scorecard detail still lives one
        level deeper, on a team's own page. Given how much complexity levels and
        weights already introduced, protecting that clarity was the right trade.
      </p>
    </Prose>

    <div class="mx-auto mt-10 max-w-2xl">
      <p class="mb-3 flex items-center gap-2 font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-wide text-[#0041d9]">
        <span aria-hidden="true">✦</span> Final design
      </p>
      <Figure
        src="/images/scorecard-gamification/leaderboard-final.png"
        caption="A podium and one team score per team, with the detail one level deeper"
        class="my-0"
      />
    </div>

    <Callout label="The trade I made explicit">
      The final leaderboard gives up inline per-scorecard detail to gain a
      comparison that reads in a second. Naming that trade-off is what let the team
      land on it with confidence, and know exactly what they'd trade to get the
      detail back later.
    </Callout>
  </CaseSection>

  <CaseSection num="05" title="What shipped">
    <Prose>
      <p>The design shipped as a connected set of capabilities:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Maturity levels</strong> — rules assigned to Below basic →
          Advanced, so teams have a level to climb rather than a flat percentage.</li>
        <li><strong>Configurable weights</strong> — compliance managers prioritize
          the rules that carry the most risk, so the score reflects what actually
          matters.</li>
        <li><strong>Team leaderboard</strong> — teams ranked by one accumulated
          score to foster healthy competition and drive operational excellence.</li>
        <li><strong>Entity progression &amp; reporting</strong> — maturity
          distribution, per-entity profiles, and rule performance over time show
          where each service stands and where to invest next.</li>
      </ul>
    </Prose>
  </CaseSection>

